  "full_response": "The SOLID principles are a set of five design principles intended to make software designs more, flexible, and maintainable. They were introduced by Robert C. Martin (also known as \"Uncle Bob\") in his book \"Agile Software Development, Principles, Patterns, and Practices.\" The five SOLID principles are:\n\n\n1. **Single Responsibility Principle (SRP)**: A class should have only one reason to change, meaning it should have only one job or responsibility. If a class has more than one purpose, it becomes more coupled and fragile.\n\n\n2. **Open/Closed Principle (OCP)**: Software entities (classes, modules, functions, etc.) should be open for extension but closed for modification. This means that the behavior of entities should be extended without modifying their source code.\n    - Read: Entities should be easy to change.\n    - Extend: New behavior or improvements can be added through inheritance or composition.\n    - Modify: The entity itself should not be modified.\n\n3. **Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP)**: Derived classes must be substitutable for their base classes. Objects of a superclass shall be replaceable with objects of a subclass without affecting the correctness of the program.\n\n4. **Interface Segregation Principle (ISP)**: No client should be forced to depend on methods it does not use. An ISP-part applies that a client can depend upon thin APIs rather than fat APIs.\n\n5. **Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP)**: High-level modules should not depend on low-level modules. Both should depend on abstractions. Additionally, abstractions should not depend upon details; details should depend upon abstractions.\n\nThese principles guide developers to create software that is easy to change and less prone to bugs, and that can easily keep up with revisions or additions. They promote the design of systems that are more modular, and that entities are designed in such a way that their behavior can be extended and maintained over time.",
